03

Experiment 2 — Connectivity Test Using Ping

Verify reachability and measure round-trip time.

ping 127.0.0.1

ping 8.8.8.8

ping -n 10 google.com

ping -t google.com

Your PC

Internet Host

8.8.8.8

Echo Request • ICMP Type 8

Echo Reply • ICMP Type 0

Reply from: host reached

time: round-trip delay

TTL: remaining hop limit

Packet loss: reliability indicator

04

Experiment 3 — Active Connections Using Netstat

Inspect TCP states, ports, PIDs and routing information.

netstat

netstat -a

netstat -n

netstat -o

netstat -ano

netstat -r

netstat -s

LISTENING

Waiting

ESTABLISHED

Active

TIME_WAIT

Closing

SYN_SENT

Request sent

Find application using a port

tasklist | findstr PID_NUMBER

Result: Active network connections and
listening ports are identified.

05

Experiments 4 & 5 — DNS Lookup and Route Tracing

Resolve names using DNS, then identify intermediate routers.

nslookup google.com

nslookup -type=mx gmail.com

tracert -d 8.8.8.8

tracert -h 15 google.com

Domain

google.com

DNS Server

IP Address

142.x.x.x

PC

R1

R2

R3

Dest.

* means a router did not respond before timeout.

06

Experiment 6 — Packet Capture Alternative to Tcpdump

Use pktmon or Wireshark on Windows to capture traffic.

pktmon start --capture --pkt-size 0

ping 8.8.8.8

pktmon stop

pktmon format PktMon.etl -o packetlog.txt

pktmon etl2pcap PktMon.etl -o packetcapture.pcapng

Capture

ETL file

Convert

PCAPNG

Open in

Wireshark

Run as Administrator for packet capture commands.

07

Experiment 7 — Capture Ping PDUs in Wireshark

Filter ICMP and inspect Echo Request / Echo Reply packets.

ping -n 4 8.8.8.8

Wireshark filter: icmp

icmp.type == 8 // Echo Request

icmp.type == 0 // Echo Reply

Frame

Ethernet

IP

ICMP

Source / destination IP

TTL and protocol

ICMP type and code

Identifier and sequence

Checksum and data payload

Match request and reply using Identifier +
Sequence Number.

